# =============================================================================
# IMPORTANT - THIS FILE IS FOR VARIABLE DISCOVERY ONLY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# build.sh scans this file ONLY to learn which variable NAMES this module
# supports. The values written here are NEVER read by the script; do not
# treat this file as a default value source.
#
# You can set the variables below using either of the following methods:
#   1. Create a '.env' file in this module directory and copy the variables
#      below into it.
#   2. Pass them directly when executing run.sh, e.g.:
#      BW_PPPOE_USERNAME=user BW_PPPOE_PASSWORD=pass ./run.sh --image=...
#
# Values are taken from (highest priority first):
#   1. Command-line environment variables (e.g. BW_PPPOE_USERNAME=user ./run.sh)
#   2. This module's .env file
#   3. The global .env file at the project root
#
# If no value is provided anywhere, the variable is replaced by an EMPTY
# string; guard your scripts with `if [ -n "$VARNAME" ]; then ... fi` so they
# safely skip when the value is empty.
#
# QUOTING rules:
#   - Command line: SINGLE-quote any value containing $, \, ` or " (double
#     quotes let your shell expand $, e.g. "pa$$w@rd" becomes pa<PID>w@rd).
#   - .env file: quotes are NOT needed. Values are read as plain text with no
#     shell expansion, so pa$$w@rd is kept verbatim. Avoid quoting values here
#     so a leading/trailing quote character in the value is never stripped.
#
# =============================================================================

# PPPoE WAN Configuration
# Set the ISP PPPoE dial-up username and password
BW_PPPOE_USERNAME=
BW_PPPOE_PASSWORD=